query pay is:
    which claim is payable.

query rn is:
    which claim is reasonable and necessary.

query stage is:
    the query fails at which section.

query dme is:
    which item is durable medical equipment.

% BioniCare Medical Technologies (13 July 2009). The BIO-1000, a device
% delivering pulsed electrical stimulation to the knee for osteoarthritis,
% rented to 47 beneficiaries on various dates in 2004 and 2005 and billed
% under E0762 (today's TEJSD code), with its supplies under A4595 (the TENS
% supply allowance); the ALJ had denied them for want of documentation that
% other treatments were tried. One beneficiary stands for the 47 (their
% dates of service are in an attachment the text does not reproduce).
% Council: not covered — the evidence (FDA clearance, the HCPCS code,
% studies by the manufacturer's own people, animal studies, prescriptions by
% 3,900 physicians) does not establish that the device was a reasonable and
% necessary treatment for osteoarthritis of the knee; it is "not a TENS
% unit"; 36 beneficiaries who signed valid ABNs are liable, the supplier for
% the other 11.
% Model: not covered — agrees. LCD L34821 now denies every E0762 claim
% (no rule concludes that one is reasonable and necessary), and an A4595
% supply is covered only with a TENS unit (E0720, E0730) covered for
% purchase, which the BIO-1000 is not: the model reads the Council's "not a
% TENS unit" from the code. No residence is stated, so the queries fail at
% applicability, one step before the question. The Council's judgment is
% about the device, not the beneficiary: no finding of the model stands for
% it, what the Council decided case by case being what the LCD now denies
% by rule. Liability under section 1879 (the ABNs) is outside the model;
% candidate template: "the beneficiary signed an advance beneficiary notice
% for *an item* stating *a reason*".
scenario bionicare is, as stated in Council decision bionicare:
    claim 1 is for the BIO-1000,
        confer "the appellant billed Medicare for the rental of the BIO-1000 under HCPCS code E0762".
    the HCPCS code of the BIO-1000 is "E0762",
        confer "the appellant billed Medicare for the rental of the BIO-1000 under HCPCS code E0762".
    the BIO-1000 is furnished to beneficiary one,
        confer "which the appellant rented to 47 beneficiaries on various dates in 2004 and 2005".
    claim 2 is for the supply kit,
        confer "for related supplies under HCPCS code A4595".
    the HCPCS code of the supply kit is "A4595",
        confer "for related supplies under HCPCS code A4595".
    the supply kit is an accessory of the BIO-1000,
        confer "Medicare Part B payment for the BIO-1000, a device that delivers pulsed electrical stimulation to the knee and is used in the treatment of osteoarthritis of the knee, and related supplies".
    the supply kit is furnished to beneficiary one,
        confer "Medicare coverage is not warranted for the BIO-1000 devices and related supplies furnished to the beneficiaries".
    the medical record of beneficiary one documents osteoarthritis of the knee,
        confer "is used in the treatment of osteoarthritis of the knee".
    rn expects answers [].
    pay expects answers [].
    stage expects answers ["the query fails at applicability"].

% M-10-979, International Rehabilitative Sciences d/b/a RS Medical (22
% September 2010). BIO-1000 devices (E0762) and supply kits (A4595)
% furnished to 28 beneficiaries in 2005, 2006 and 2007 (some paid, then
% questioned for their fee); the ALJ, on remand, found the device
% experimental and investigational. One beneficiary stands for the 28.
% Council: not covered — as in BioniCare: FDA clearance and a HCPCS code do
% not show the device reasonable and necessary, the studies are the
% sponsor's, the animal studies have no probative value, the physicians'
% prescriptions and the contractors' payments are not general acceptance;
% the device is "not a TENS unit"; the Almy decision (D. Md. 2010) is
% persuasive, International Rehabilitative Sciences v. Sebelius (W.D. Wash.
% 2010) limited to its facts. Ten beneficiaries with valid ABNs are liable;
% the supplier for the other eighteen (no ABN, or an ABN "equivocal" on
% coverage).
% Model: not covered — agrees, as in BioniCare (no rule covers E0762; the
% A4595 kit needs a covered TENS unit; no residence stated, so the queries
% fail at applicability). The fee schedule question (80 percent of the
% charge before 1 July 2007) is pricing, outside the model.
scenario international_rehabilitative_sciences is, as stated in Council decision international rehabilitative sciences:
    claim 1 is for the BIO-1000,
        confer "The appellant billed Medicare for the BIO-1000s under HCPCS code E0762 and for the supply kits under A4595".
    the HCPCS code of the BIO-1000 is "E0762",
        confer "The appellant billed Medicare for the BIO-1000s under HCPCS code E0762".
    the BIO-1000 is furnished to beneficiary one,
        confer "BIO-1000 devices and/or supply kits it furnished to 28 Medicare beneficiaries in 2005, 2006, and 2007".
    claim 2 is for the supply kit,
        confer "for the supply kits under A4595".
    the HCPCS code of the supply kit is "A4595",
        confer "for the supply kits under A4595".
    the supply kit is an accessory of the BIO-1000,
        confer "whether the record as a whole supports a finding that the BIO-1000 and, if applicable, any related supplies, were reasonable and necessary".
    the supply kit is furnished to beneficiary one,
        confer "BIO-1000 devices and/or supply kits it furnished to 28 Medicare beneficiaries in 2005, 2006, and 2007".
    the medical record of beneficiary one documents osteoarthritis of the knee,
        confer "The BIO-1000 is a device that delivers pulsed electrical stimulation to the knee and is used in the treatment of osteoarthritis of the knee".
    rn expects answers [].
    pay expects answers [].
    stage expects answers ["the query fails at applicability"].

% Kinetic Concepts (28 October 2008): an ORDER remanding to the ALJ the
% claims for the VAC pump (E2402) and its supplies furnished to 46
% beneficiaries between January and October 2006, which the ALJ had denied
% because the supplier's checklist forms lacked medical detail.
% Council: remand, no decision on coverage — the checklist forms, completed
% by a provider unaffiliated with the supplier and carrying the wound
% measurements, suffice for the continued coverage of the first four
% months (LCD L11500), unlike the bare certificates of Maximum Comfort; they
% do not suffice beyond four months, which needs additional documentation;
% shallow wounds are not excluded as such. The one claim the decision
% describes individually, of beneficiary E.M.R. residing in a skilled
% nursing facility on the date of service, 2 May 2006, the ALJ denied on
% that ground, and "would appear to be correct"; the Council deferred it.
% Model: not payable — agrees with the Council's remark: a skilled nursing
% facility is not a home (42 CFR 410.38), so the claim fails at
% applicability. The wound, its type and the start of the therapy are not
% stated, so the claim is not reasonable and necessary either. The
% Council's reading of the checklist forms is the model's too: the monthly
% healing assessment the supplier obtains from the treating clinician
% (Policy Article A52511, "The supplier need not view the medical records")
% is a fact of the record, not a medical record the supplier must hold. The
% monthly rental is not numbered in the decision, and is not transcribed.
scenario kinetic_concepts08 is, as stated in Council decision kinetic concepts 08:
    claim 1 is for the VAC,
        confer "the single claim for beneficiary E.M.R.".
    the HCPCS code of the VAC is "E2402",
        confer "The VAC device (HCPCS Code E2402)".
    the VAC is furnished to EMR,
        confer "the single claim for beneficiary E.M.R.".
    the date of service of claim 1 is 2006-05-02,
        confer "the claimed date of service, May 2, 2006".
    the residence of EMR is skilled nursing facility,
        confer "the beneficiary’s residence in a skilled nursing facility on the claimed date of service".
    rn expects answers [].
    pay expects answers [].
    stage expects answers ["the query fails at applicability"].

% Kinetic Concepts (5 June 2009). VAC pumps (E2402) and supplies for 19
% beneficiaries whose therapy extended beyond four months, and canisters in
% excess of ten a month for one of them (Beneficiary 65), between May 2005
% and January 2007. Two beneficiaries the decision describes: Beneficiary
% 50, whose dates of service of 24 and 26 October 2006 precede the clinic
% notes of November 2006 (the first is transcribed, the item of the second
% not being named), and Beneficiary 65, who used the portable Freedom VAC.
% Council: not covered — coverage ends after four months unless
% "additional documentation" — from independent providers, case specific,
% contemporaneous with the fifth month, explaining the special
% circumstances and justifying the expense — shows the need; the checklist
% forms and letters of medical necessity ("Wound healing", "Continue for 1
% more month") do not; Beneficiary 50's wound, moreover, showed no
% measurable healing between cycles 13 and 14. The canisters beyond ten a
% month are not covered with a portable pump: "For high volume exudative
% wounds, a stationary pump with the largest capacity canister must be
% used". The supplier is liable.
% Model: neither claim reasonable and necessary — agrees on the outcome,
% not on the grounds: the decision states neither wound's type, stage or
% age, nor the date NPWT started, so neither initial criterion A or B nor
% the four months (read by npwt.le from the start of therapy, 120 days) can
% be decided; the "cycles 13 and 14" suggest the fourteenth monthly rental
% but the decision does not say that a cycle is a month, and the rental
% month is not transcribed. The individual consideration beyond four months
% through appeals (Policy Article A52511) is not modelled: no rule covers a
% pump after 120 days whatever the documentation. The canisters: no quantity
% is stated ("in excess of ten a month") nor the drainage; and npwt.le's
% rule for more than ten canisters reads only the drainage (over 90 ml a
% day), not the stationary pump the current LCD L33821 still requires — on
% Beneficiary 65's facts with a documented drainage the model would cover
% the canisters the Council denied. Candidate templates: "*a device* is a
% stationary pump", "NPWT for *a wound* has been used for *a number*
% months", "the additional documentation for *a claim* explains the special
% circumstances necessitating the extended therapy", "the quantity billed
% on *a claim* exceeds *a number*".
scenario kinetic_concepts09 is, as stated in Council decision kinetic concepts 09:
    claim 1 is for the VAC,
        confer "those dates of service, October 24 and October 26, 2006 are not covered because the case files lack additional documentation".
    the HCPCS code of the VAC is "E2402",
        confer "The VAC device (HCPCS Code E2402)".
    the VAC is furnished to beneficiary fifty,
        confer "Two dates of service for beneficiary 50 precede the dates of the Clinic Notes".
    the date of service of claim 1 is 2006-10-24,
        confer "those dates of service, October 24 and October 26, 2006 are not covered because the case files lack additional documentation".
    the VAC is applied to the orthopaedic wound,
        confer "He continues on his VAC dressing".
    the orthopaedic wound is a wound of beneficiary fifty,
        confer "the record contains three “File Notes” within the “Orthopaedics Clinic Note:”".
    a licensed medical professional directly assesses the orthopaedic wound and supervises the NPWT dressing changes,
        confer "His VAC dressing was changed".
    claim 2 is for the canisters,
        confer "the excess canisters provided to Beneficiary 65 are not covered".
    the HCPCS code of the canisters is "A7000",
        confer "canisters for the collection of fluid (HCPCS Codes A6551 and A7000)".
    the canisters is furnished to beneficiary sixty five,
        confer "the excess canisters provided to Beneficiary 65 are not covered".
    the canisters is an accessory of the Freedom VAC,
        confer "the beneficiary was not using the stationary pump but instead was using the portable Freedom VAC pump".
    the HCPCS code of the Freedom VAC is "E2402",
        confer "The VAC device (HCPCS Code E2402), which may be prescribed as either a standard or portable model".
    the Freedom VAC is furnished to beneficiary sixty five,
        confer "the beneficiary was not using the stationary pump but instead was using the portable Freedom VAC pump".
    rn expects answers [].
    pay expects answers [].
    stage expects answers ["the query fails at applicability"].

% M-13-751, Zoll LifeCor (21 August 2013). The rental of a wearable
% defibrillator, the LifeVest (K0606), delivered on 17 September 2010 to a
% hospital inpatient discharged home on 20 September; order form dated 14
% September 2010, signed 17 September, with "unknown" for the scheduled
% discharge date and no initial date of need; ejection fraction 35 percent,
% a cardiomyopathy documented after the date of service, ventricular
% tachycardia. The supplier asked that the date of service be the date of
% discharge.
% Council: not covered — on the date of service the beneficiary was a
% hospital inpatient, and a hospital is not a home (42 CFR 410.38(b)); the
% conditions under which DME may be delivered before discharge (Claims
% Processing Manual ch. 20 §110.3.1) are not met: no initial date of need on
% the order, delivery three days (not two) before discharge, payment
% claimed for a day before discharge; nothing in the record shows a planned
% discharge date that was delayed. The supplier is liable.
% Model: not payable — agrees: the residence on the date of service is a
% hospital, so the claim fails at applicability. The model finds it
% reasonable and necessary (criterion 3 of LCD L33690: a dilated
% cardiomyopathy and an ejection fraction of 0.35 or less), where the ALJ
% found the coverage requirements not met because the cardiomyopathy was
% documented after the date of service; the Council rested on the place of
% use and did not decide that. The model's documented conditions and its
% residence carry no date: the discharge home three days later cannot be
% stated beside the hospital without letting the claim through, nor the
% date of the cardiomyopathy's documentation. The two-day pre-discharge
% delivery rule is not in the model. Candidate templates: "*a beneficiary*
% was an inpatient of *a place* from *a date* to *an other date*", "the
% medical record of *a beneficiary* documents *a condition* on *a date*",
% "the order for *an item* states an initial date of need of *a date*".
scenario m_13_751 is, as stated in Council decision m 13 751:
    claim 1 is for the LifeVest,
        confer "the rental of a wearable automatic external defibrillator (HCPCS code K0606), furnished to the beneficiary on September 17, 2010".
    the HCPCS code of the LifeVest is "K0606",
        confer "the rental of a wearable automatic external defibrillator (HCPCS code K0606), furnished to the beneficiary on September 17, 2010".
    the LifeVest is furnished to the beneficiary,
        confer "the rental of a wearable automatic external defibrillator (HCPCS code K0606), furnished to the beneficiary on September 17, 2010".
    the date of service of claim 1 is 2010-09-17,
        confer "which the record consistently states is the date of service for this claim".
    the rental month of claim 1 is 1,
        confer "At issue before the Council is Medicare coverage for the rental of a wearable automatic external defibrillator (AED), the LifeVest®, furnished to the beneficiary on September 17, 2010".
    the residence of the beneficiary is hospital,
        confer "on the date of service, the beneficiary was an inpatient in a hospital".
    the medical record of the beneficiary documents dilated cardiomyopathy,
        confer "confirming diagnosis of dilated cardiomyopathy".
    the measured left ventricular ejection fraction of the beneficiary is 0.35,
        confer "an echocardiogram (ECG) indicated an ejection fraction of 35 percent".
    the order for the LifeVest is dated 2010-09-14,
        confer "a physician signed Zoll LifeCor’s order form, dated September 14, 2010".
    the order for the LifeVest is signed by the treating practitioner,
        confer "The record reflects that, on September 17, 2010, a physician signed Zoll LifeCor’s order form".
    the order for the LifeVest includes description of the item,
        confer "order form, dated September 14, 2010, for the LifeVest®".
    the order for the LifeVest was communicated to the supplier on 2010-09-17,
        confer "The appellant furnished the item on the day on which the physician signed the form".
    the LifeVest was delivered on 2010-09-17,
        confer "there is no dispute as to the date of delivery, which was September 17, 2010".
    rn expects answers ["claim 1 is reasonable and necessary"].
    pay expects answers [].
    stage expects answers ["the query fails at applicability"].

% M-11-1779, S.M. (17 December 2012). A diabetic alert dog billed under
% E1399 ("durable medical equipment, miscellaneous"), provided on 14 April
% 2009 and claimed by the beneficiary's attorney-in-fact on 7 August 2009;
% the beneficiary is under a physician's care for diabetes, and the
% physician thinks the dog in his best interest.
% Council: not covered (the ALJ's decision adopted) — the dog is not
% durable medical equipment: an animal trained to detect a drop in blood
% glucose does not, by constant companionship, primarily and customarily
% serve a medical purpose, and may be useful to a person in the absence of
% illness or injury; NCD 280.1's reference list has no such item. The
% reasonable and necessary question is not reached.
% Model: not covered — agrees. E1399 belongs to no modelled policy, so the
% claim is outside the model's scope (the query fails at applicability);
% and the dog is not durable medical equipment by dmepos.le's definition,
% none of the four characteristics being stated (the two the Council
% discussed it denied; the other two, repeated use and use in the home, it
% did not address). A characteristic denied cannot be stated as such:
% candidate templates "*an item* is primarily and customarily used for a
% nonmedical purpose", "*an item* is useful to a person in the absence of
% illness or injury" (the negations of dmepos.le's characteristics).
scenario m_11_1779 is, as stated in Council decision m 11 1779:
    claim 1 is for the diabetic alert dog,
        confer "a diabetic alert dog (HCPCS code E1399) provided to the beneficiary on April 14, 2009".
    the HCPCS code of the diabetic alert dog is "E1399",
        confer "a diabetic alert dog (HCPCS code E1399) provided to the beneficiary on April 14, 2009".
    the diabetic alert dog is furnished to SM,
        confer "a diabetic alert dog (HCPCS code E1399) provided to the beneficiary on April 14, 2009".
    the date of service of claim 1 is 2009-04-14,
        confer "a diabetic alert dog (HCPCS code E1399) provided to the beneficiary on April 14, 2009".
    claim 1 was submitted on 2009-08-07,
        confer "dated August 7, 2009, on behalf of the beneficiary for a diabetic alert dog".
    the medical record of SM documents diabetes,
        confer "The beneficiary is under a physician's care for diabetes".
    rn expects answers [].
    pay expects answers [].
    stage expects answers ["the query fails at applicability"].
    dme expects answers [].

% Hudson Home Health Care (12 August 2009). An ultralightweight manual
% wheelchair (code not stated; K0005 is assumed) and accessories provided
% on 25 June 2008 to a 38-year-old woman of 300 pounds with paraplegia from
% transverse myelitis, whose five-year-old wheelchair was broken beyond
% repair and below her weight; a letter of 30 March 2008 from a
% rehabilitation hospital, signed by her physical therapist and her
% physician, gives her strength (upper extremities 4-/5, lower 2+/5), her
% independent propulsion of an ultralight wheelchair up to 400 feet, and
% her inability to propel a standard or lightweight wheelchair for weakness
% and fatigue. The ALJ had excluded late evidence and read the letter as
% the therapist's alone.
% Council: covered — the letter, signed by both, establishes the medical
% necessity of the ultralightweight wheelchair under LCD L11465, which gave
% such chairs "individual consideration"; nobody questioned her need of a
% wheelchair, only of a replacement and its features.
% DISAGREES: the model finds the claim not reasonable and necessary — the
% current LCD L33788 covers a K0005 only with a specialty evaluation by a
% professional with no financial relationship with the supplier and a
% RESNA-certified ATP directly involved in the selection (criteria (3) and
% (4)), which the decision does not state (the evaluating therapist is the
% rehabilitation hospital's; her relationship with the supplier is not
% said) and which the "individual consideration" of L11465, as the Council
% read it, did not require. With those two facts the answer would be
% conditional on the home (C), improvement (D) and willingness (E)
% findings, which nobody made. Not payable also for want of a residence and
% an order. Candidate templates: "*an item* replaces equipment broken
% beyond repair", "the weight capacity of *an item* is *a number* pounds"
% (the old chair's limit, below her 300 pounds).
scenario hudson_home_healthcare is, as stated in Council decision hudson home healthcare:
    claim 1 is for the wheelchair,
        confer "an ultra lightweight wheelchair and accessories provided to the beneficiary on June 25, 2008".
    the HCPCS code of the wheelchair is "K0005",
        confer "the ultra lightweight wheelchair the beneficiary obtained on June 25, 2008".
    the wheelchair is furnished to the beneficiary,
        confer "an ultra lightweight wheelchair and accessories provided to the beneficiary on June 25, 2008".
    the date of service of claim 1 is 2008-06-25,
        confer "an ultra lightweight wheelchair and accessories provided to the beneficiary on June 25, 2008".
    the wheelchair was delivered on 2008-06-25,
        confer "the ultra lightweight wheelchair the beneficiary obtained on June 25, 2008".
    the weight of the beneficiary is 300 pounds,
        confer "her present weight of 300 pounds exceeds the weight limit for her previous wheelchair".
    the finding significant mobility limitation about the beneficiary is established,
        according to the Council, because "she has transverse myelitis with resulting paraplegia, and none of the decisionmakers questioned whether she requires a wheelchair",
        confer "None of the decisionmakers in this case has questioned whether the beneficiary requires a wheelchair".
    the finding walking aids insufficient about the beneficiary is established,
        according to the Council, because "she has transverse myelitis with resulting paraplegia, and none of the decisionmakers questioned whether she requires a wheelchair",
        confer "has transverse myelitis with resulting paraplegia".
    the finding manual wheelchair can be propelled about the beneficiary is established,
        according to the treating practitioner,
        confer "is independent with propelling an ultra light wheelchair for distances up to 400 feet".
    the finding ultralightweight wheelchair justified about the beneficiary is established,
        according to the Council, because "she is unable to functionally propel a standard manual wheelchair or lightweight wheelchair due to weakness and fatigue",
        confer "is sufficient to establish the medical necessity of the ultra lightweight wheelchair the beneficiary obtained on June 25, 2008".
    rn expects answers [].
    pay expects answers [].
    stage expects answers ["the query fails at applicability"].

% Maximum Comfort (11 June 2003, own-motion review). Power-operated
% wheelchairs (code not stated; the Group 2 standard chair K0823 is assumed,
% as in council_pmd.le) and accessories supplied in 1998 and 1999 to the
% beneficiaries of two statistical samples (the H. group, lead beneficiary
% B.H., and the T. group), paid and then found overpaid on post-payment
% review; the ALJs found them covered on the certificates of medical
% necessity the ordering physicians signed. For one beneficiary the CMN's
% section B was completed by his wife, and the prescribing physician's
% note of 26 January 1999 says he walks with a walker.
% Council: not covered — a CMN alone does not establish medical necessity:
% the DMERC supplier manual and a 1997 newsletter required the supplier to
% keep supporting medical documentation, and the CMN did not even ask
% whether the patient would otherwise be bed or chair confined; the record
% holds none (claim 1, B.H.). The beneficiary who ambulates with a walker
% does not meet the coverage requirements for a power-operated wheelchair
% (claim 2). The supplier knew or should have known (section 1879), and is
% not without fault (section 1870).
% Model: neither claim reasonable and necessary — agrees on the outcome.
% Claim 2 fails on the walker (B unsupported). Claim 1 fails only because
% the weight is not stated: the model reads an unrecorded finding as an
% open question, not as a denial, so with a weight the answer would be
% reasonable and necessary conditional on the eight findings — where the
% Council reads the absence of documentation behind the CMN as the denial.
% The certificate of medical necessity itself has no place in the model
% (the standard written order and the findings replace it). Candidate
% templates: "the certificate of medical necessity for *an item* was
% completed by *a person*", "the medical record supports the certificate of
% medical necessity for *an item*".
scenario maximum_comfort is, as stated in Council decision maximum comfort:
    claim 1 is for the power wheelchair of BH,
        confer "a group of cases with B.H. designated as the lead beneficiary".
    the HCPCS code of the power wheelchair of BH is "K0823",
        confer "the power-operated wheelchairs and accessories which were provided to the Medicare beneficiaries between January 1, 1998 and January 22, 1999".
    the power wheelchair of BH is furnished to BH,
        confer "the power-operated wheelchairs and accessories which were provided to the Medicare beneficiaries between January 1, 1998 and January 22, 1999".
    the order for the power wheelchair of BH is signed by the treating practitioner,
        confer "certificates of medical necessity (CMNs) provided by the beneficiaries’ physicians, who also ordered the equipment".
    claim 2 is for the power wheelchair of the beneficiary,
        confer "The Appellant provided a power-operated wheelchair and accessories to beneficiary *** that was not medically reasonable and necessary".
    the HCPCS code of the power wheelchair of the beneficiary is "K0823",
        confer "the Administrative Law Judge’s conclusion that the medical records provided supported the medical necessity of the power-operated wheelchair is not supported by the evidence of record".
    the power wheelchair of the beneficiary is furnished to the beneficiary,
        confer "The Appellant provided a power-operated wheelchair and accessories to beneficiary *** that was not medically reasonable and necessary".
    the order for the power wheelchair of the beneficiary is signed by the treating practitioner,
        confer "The clinical record for a visit to the prescribing physician".
    the finding walking aids insufficient about the beneficiary is unsupported,
        according to the Council, because "the clinical record of a visit to the prescribing physician on January 26, 1999 states that the beneficiary is ambulating with a walker",
        confer "because the beneficiary was able to ambulate with a walker".
    rn expects answers [].
    pay expects answers [].
    stage expects answers ["the query fails at applicability"].

% M-10-579, J.D.K. (20 August 2010). A Vitrectomy Solutions DayTimer
% positioning system — a seated system with a face-down head support —
% rented for two weeks from 6 November 2008 after surgery of 4 November
% 2008 for a macular hole, on a CMN for a face-down support system ("Patient
% must maintain face down position 45 minutes every hour"); the beneficiary
% filed the claim himself on 2 December 2008, and the contractor coded it
% E0190 (positioning cushion) and denied it as not primarily medical; the
% QIC and the ALJ found it miscoded ("not a cushion but a chair") and could
% not change the code.
% Council: covered — the item meets the definition of DME (rented, usable
% by successive patients; immobilizing the head face down so the gas bubble
% is not dislodged is a medical purpose; not generally used absent illness
% or injury; appropriate for use in the home) and was reasonable and
% necessary for the two weeks; the contractor, which assigned E0190, is to
% recode it to the appropriate descriptive or miscellaneous code and pay.
% No limitation of liability applies to an unassigned claim.
% DISAGREES: the model finds the claim not covered — neither the support
% surface policies (support_surfaces.le: the Group 1-3 mattresses, overlays
% and beds) nor the wheelchair seating policy (wheelchair_seating.le:
% cushions and positioning accessories of a wheelchair) decides E0190, nor
% would any decide a miscellaneous code (E1399), so the claim is outside the
% model's scope and the query fails at applicability. The item IS durable
% medical equipment by dmepos.le's definition — the four characteristics the
% Council found are stated — but that definition places an item in the
% benefit only; no rule of the model says when an item that "does not fall
% logically into any of the generic categories" of NCD 280.1 is reasonable
% and necessary for the individual patient. Candidate: a rule for such an
% item reading a finding "item reasonable and necessary for individual
% patient" (NCD 280.1's individual determination), and "*a code* was
% assigned to *a claim* by the contractor".
scenario jdk is, as stated in Council decision jdk:
    claim 1 is for the positioning system,
        confer "Vitrectomy Solutions DayTimer positioning system".
    the HCPCS code of the positioning system is "E0190",
        confer "it was incorrectly billed as a positioning cushion (billing code E0190)".
    the positioning system is furnished to JDK,
        confer "The beneficiary ordered and received, for a rental period of two weeks beginning on November 6, 2008".
    the date of service of claim 1 is 2008-11-06,
        confer "The beneficiary ordered and received, for a rental period of two weeks beginning on November 6, 2008".
    the rental month of claim 1 is 1,
        confer "for a rental period of two weeks beginning on November 6, 2008".
    claim 1 was submitted on 2008-12-02,
        confer "filed a claim, dated December 2, 2008".
    the medical record of JDK documents macular hole,
        confer "The beneficiary received surgery on November 4, 2008 to treat a macular hole related to macular degeneration".
    the order for the positioning system is signed by the treating practitioner,
        confer "issued a Certificate of Medical Necessity (CMN) for a face-down support system for a period of two weeks".
    the order for the positioning system includes description of the item,
        confer "issued a Certificate of Medical Necessity (CMN) for a face-down support system for a period of two weeks".
    the positioning system can withstand repeated use,
        confer "could be used by successive patients".
    the positioning system is primarily and customarily used to serve a medical purpose,
        confer "This is a medical purpose".
    the positioning system is generally not useful to a person in the absence of illness or injury,
        confer "this item would not generally be used in the absence of illness or injury".
    the positioning system is appropriate for use in the home,
        confer "was certainly appropriate for use in the patient’s home".
    rn expects answers [].
    pay expects answers [].
    stage expects answers ["the query fails at applicability"].
    dme expects answers ["the positioning system is durable medical equipment"].
